        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "ec23cc8c-7933-36f5-a137-441cb267f601",
                "word": "cancérigène",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "33901dc7-eb70-461a-a465-594d9beebcd8",
                        "entry_id": "ec23cc8c-7933-36f5-a137-441cb267f601",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "cancérigènes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d6ee93b7-23cf-3a6d-8a33-f04cfb9854ca",
                        "entry_id": "ec23cc8c-7933-36f5-a137-441cb267f601",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Caractérise une substance qui peut favoriser l’apparition d’un cancer ; synonyme dans ce sens de cancérogène."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "common"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4f37f3c-3e82-4ecd-a79b-b61fd0ad8e48",
                                "sense_id": "d6ee93b7-23cf-3a6d-8a33-f04cfb9854ca",
                                "text": "Le matériau utilisé pour la fabrication de ces poêles, le Pfoa (acide perfluorooctanoïque) — interdit par l’Union européenne en juillet 2020 — pourrait s’avérer cancérigène et, d’après le naturopathe, modifierait même notre ADN."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5e557808-727d-31aa-9a53-74c2112c6a16",
                        "entry_id": "ec23cc8c-7933-36f5-a137-441cb267f601",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui peut aggraver ou sensibiliser un cancer."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Moins courant"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "de75232c-d2f2-4e64-b997-39fdc6e4915e",
                                "sense_id": "5e557808-727d-31aa-9a53-74c2112c6a16",
                                "text": "Pour l’académie de médecine, cancérigène est utilisé au sujet d'une substance favorisant le développement d’un cancer. Quant à cancérogène, ce qui est plus conforme à son étymologie, il désigne une substance favorisant l'apparition d'un cancer"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "265007b1-30d4-4c55-9e1c-f780037d552f",
                                "sense_id": "5e557808-727d-31aa-9a53-74c2112c6a16",
                                "text": "La classification des mélanges comme cancérigènes, mutagènes ou toxiques pour la reproduction doit être calculée sur la base des informations disponibles relatives aux substances présentes dans le mélange."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "555dae53-51bb-43b1-a739-6f92411fc05f",
                        "source_entry_id": "ec23cc8c-7933-36f5-a137-441cb267f601",
                        "source_sense_id": "d6ee93b7-23cf-3a6d-8a33-f04cfb9854ca",
                        "source_sense_description": "Sens médical",
                        "target_lang_word": "carcinogenic",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "carcinogenic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b07cd4f9-f224-3cd4-b192-05daf16f753e",
                                        "entry_id": "c6928de4-394b-385f-b048-7a47fad3605f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Causing or tending to cause cancer."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d809af9-5024-4998-824f-45f526f99bb4",
                                                "sense_id": "b07cd4f9-f224-3cd4-b192-05daf16f753e",
                                                "text": "Although BHA (butylated hydroxyanisole) and BHT (butylated hydroxytoluene) are avoided by some consumers because several studies have suggested they may be toxic or carcinogenic (they are banned in Britain), these antioxidants have also been shown to inhibit the action of cancer-causing chemicals, and BHT has been shown to suppress the growth of harmful viruses."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "607ee078-8675-4de7-894b-d3d835ccdb65",
                                                "sense_id": "b07cd4f9-f224-3cd4-b192-05daf16f753e",
                                                "text": "The disadvantage of the underfired grill is the potential for flareups, which can cause a burned spot or carbonization. Not only do these taste bad, but they are also carcinogenic."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
